The competitive claw machine boom

How a childhood obsession became an online phenomenon and a full-time caree=
r for claw-machine enthusiasts.

BY ANNA RAHMANAN (https://www.instagram.com/annabyrahmanan/?hl=3Den&_hsenc=
=3Dp2ANqtz--mUX4lkbeDPM9BY5uNcQiwpmR3ynWyTMnNiffcpyNxGw6m354_HN_m4O0cO2jM7q=
9RxGm_42HO-yle8qvL9BbJbxNYLg&_hsmi=3D397763044 )

Walk into almost any American mall, bowling alley, or family restaurant and=
 you=E2=80=99ll see it: a glowing box of plush toys and blinking LEDs waiti=
ng for someone to drop a few dollars to try to grab a prize.

Claw machines could have been relics, lost to the =E2=80=9890s alongside sp=
itball shooters and mall photobooths. Instead, they=E2=80=99ve amassed bill=
ions of views on social media and have proved to be a viable small business=
 for local operators.

Their popularity isn=E2=80=99t from winning expensive prizes, or because co=
mpetitions pay big. Claw machines are still difficult to win and many belie=
ve they=E2=80=99re rigged by operators. It=E2=80=99s because claw machines =
=E2=80=94 in their own way, nostalgia personified =E2=80=94 have become an =
unlikely and effective modern vehicle for monetizable content.

A Vogue editor plays on a claw machine at a fashion event in Sydney, Austra=
lia. (Photo by Mark Metcalfe/Getty Images for Vaefno)

And in the center of this unexpected industry is a generation of players an=
d operators who treat claw machines not as games, but as engines for making=
 money.

The kids who never grew out of it

Before claw machines were content, before they were side hustles, before th=
ey were tropes in TikTok videos, they were everywhere and irresistible to E=
rik Kane.

=E2=80=9CI remember always seeing them in the early 2000s growing up,=E2=80=
=9D says Kane, the 26-year-old creator behind the YouTube account Arcade Wa=
rrior (https://www.youtube.com/channel/UClRVvM7X7zX65HY9P7Zllvg?_hsenc=3Dp2=
ANqtz--mUX4lkbeDPM9BY5uNcQiwpmR3ynWyTMnNiffcpyNxGw6m354_HN_m4O0cO2jM7q9RxGm=
_42HO-yle8qvL9BbJbxNYLg&_hsmi=3D397763044 ) . =E2=80=9CThere was always one=
 in a grocery store, restaurant, mall. They were very prevalent. The marque=
e sign would light up next to flashing lights, plus the claw itself. I was =
always drawn to it.=E2=80=9D

As a kid in Rochester, New York, Kane begged his parents for a dollar to pl=
ay. "If it was not for them giving me a dollar to play whenever I would see=
 one, I probably never would have gotten fully invested in them," he says. =
Eventually, he started filming. Today, his channel has 2m+ subscribers.

=E2=80=9CAround the time I was 12 or 13, I started to film some clips of me=
 just playing,=E2=80=9D he says. =E2=80=9CMe and my brother would go around=
 and film but wouldn=E2=80=99t upload anything. It was just something we di=
d for fun.=E2=80=9D

Erik Kane shows off his latest plushie prize. (YouTube)

In Pennsylvania, future YouTuber Matt Magnone, known online as Arcade Matt =
(https://www.youtube.com/@arcade_matt?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz--mUX4lkbeDPM9BY5uNcQ=
iwpmR3ynWyTMnNiffcpyNxGw6m354_HN_m4O0cO2jM7q9RxGm_42HO-yle8qvL9BbJbxNYLg&_h=
smi=3D397763044 ) , was doing the same thing.

=E2=80=9CEver since I was a little kid, it was the one game at the arcade t=
hat you could win an instant gift with,=E2=80=9D says the 38-year-old. =E2=
=80=9CInstant gratification=E2=80=94millennials love that. I didn=E2=80=99t=
 care what the prize was. It was just the fact that I could win something.=
=E2=80=9D

At 17, he bought a full-size claw machine for $500, which he stored in his =
mom=E2=80=99s basement and practiced on daily.

=E2=80=9CMy first job was a cashier at a neighborhood pharmacy and I only m=
ade $5 an hour so I had to save up for months and months and months in my p=
retzel jar until I finally had enough money to buy it,=E2=80=9D he says.

He now has 1.83m YouTube subscribers, who he fondly refers to as goobers.

Alongside the channels, the competitive claw machine community has grown. A=
spiring clawmasters share tips and tricks online. Clawcades host tournament=
s for local players. The cost to play depends on location and prizes, but o=
n average is still between a quarter and $2 per play. They=E2=80=99re espec=
ially popular in Japan, where a woman named Yuka Nakajima holds the Guinnes=
s World Record for most claw-machine wins at 3.5k.

Matt Magnone says winning claw tournaments isn=E2=80=99t where the revenue =
is =E2=80=93 it=E2=80=99s in videos of him playing the game. (Courtesy of M=
att Magnone)

And like any community, it has its own drama. Magnone explains he=E2=80=99s=
 called out members online who =E2=80=9Cgive false information on hacks=E2=
=80=9D to gather views.

=E2=80=9CThat ruins it for us content creators because the arcades see us w=
alking in with a camera and think we want to try to hack the system,=E2=80=
=9D he says.

He=E2=80=99s called out arcades, too, for allegedly rigging their games (ht=
tps://www.vox.com/2015/4/3/8339999/claw-machines-rigged?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz--m=
UX4lkbeDPM9BY5uNcQiwpmR3ynWyTMnNiffcpyNxGw6m354_HN_m4O0cO2jM7q9RxGm_42HO-yl=
e8qvL9BbJbxNYLg&_hsmi=3D397763044 ) or keeping payout levels low. (Anecdota=
lly, machines will pay out between 10%-30% of the time, but it depends on h=
ow the owner has set the game.)

Videos cost Magnone between $50-$100, and throughout his career he=E2=80=99=
s amassed a wide range of prizes: from mp4 players (=E2=80=9Cthey were very=
 valuable then=E2=80=9D) to licensed items like Minecraft-related products,=
 giant Squishable plushies and more. Once, he turned $20 into 33 prizes at =
Dave & Busters.

=E2=80=9CI don=E2=80=99t really do any kind of training,=E2=80=9D he says. =
=E2=80=9CClaw machines are one of those things that, once you get the feel =
for it, you just have to catch the machine at the right time and the prize =
in the right position.=E2=80=9D

The jackpot behind the jackpot

Neither Kane nor Magnone make money winning tournament jackpots. The real o=
pportunity, the thing that birthed what could be described as the claw econ=
omy, is that claw machines translate perfectly into content.

What makes claw-machine content so sticky online is that it=E2=80=99s built=
 on the same psychological loop as the gameplay itself: set up, suspense, a=
nd then payoff.

The viewer experiences the =E2=80=9Calmost win=E2=80=9D on screen, without =
paying for the next attempt, watching the claw strains to latch onto the pr=
ize. It creates a kind of low-grade high: the thrill of almost.

Plushies based on licensed characters, like the ones above in an arcade in =
Italy, can help drive traffic to a machine. (Photo by Matteo Della Torre/Nu=
rPhoto via Getty Images)

It=E2=80=99s no surprise, then, that, when the audience is in the millions,=
 a $1 plush toy =E2=80=94 or the mere chance of clawing a $1 toy from a mac=
hine =E2=80=94 can generate thousands in ad revenue.

One thing that Magnone and Kane do have to keep in mind, though, is the ris=
k of being repetitive.

=E2=80=9CI honestly feel like my content is very repetitive,=E2=80=9D says =
Magnone.

He tries to stay on top of trends to skirt around an algorithm that rewards=
 him for constantly posting similar content. =E2=80=9CWhen fidget spinners =
were hot, that=E2=80=99s what propelled me to over a million subscribers be=
cause there was a claw machine with one in it and I won it,=E2=80=9D he rem=
embers.

=E2=80=9CBut it=E2=80=99s hard to always find machines filled with items th=
at are trending so you just have to go with the flow.=E2=80=9D

The evolution of the machines

Part of the enduring appeal is that claw machines still invite debate about=
 fairness, skill, and rigging. In reality, the technology has evolved in wa=
ys that make the entire system both more sophisticated and more predictable=
.

=E2=80=9CIt=E2=80=99s not a myth but a common misconception,=E2=80=9D Magno=
ne says. =E2=80=9CCertain machines are 100% skill-based.=E2=80=9D

Machines in the =E2=80=9880s and =E2=80=9890s had knobs inside them that al=
lowed owners to set the strength of a claw=E2=80=99s grip or the number of =
times a claw would drop a prize before it delivered it to its winner.

More modern versions of the tool are different.

=E2=80=9CNowadays, the machines are filled with very expensive prizes: vide=
o games, handbags,=E2=80=9D Kane says. =E2=80=9CThose machines are usually =
set on a payout rate. If an item costs $20, until $20 or more is put into t=
he machine, it=E2=80=99s not going to pay out.=E2=80=9D

The thrill of the almost-win keeps players (and viewers) coming back. (Phot=
o by Calla Kessler/The Washington Post via Getty Images)

According to Magnone, older claw machines were largely skill-based, with a =
fixed claw strength that stayed consistent from play to play and allowed ex=
perienced players to win through good positioning and technique.

Newer machines, by contrast, often use payout systems that tie claw strengt=
h to how much money has been fed into the game, meaning the claw only reach=
es full strength after the operator=E2=80=99s preset threshold is met. They=
=E2=80=99re usually flashier, featuring LED lights, loud music and sleeker =
designs, and they make it much harder for the average player to tell when a=
 prize is actually winnable.

Magnone can detect the difference on sight. =E2=80=9CLook for the credit di=
splay, the way the joystick moves,=E2=80=9D he suggests. =E2=80=9CThe older=
 machines that let you only go forward once and then right, those were all =
skill. They don=E2=80=99t make those anymore.=E2=80=9D

=E2=80=9CNow that I=E2=80=99m on the other side, I play less=E2=80=9D

Based in Melbourne, Australia, Jack Ennis owns and operates ~40 claw machin=
es across trampoline parks, kids=E2=80=99 play centers, malls and restauran=
ts. He started as a player and eventually became an operator because he rea=
lized the business was shockingly profitable.

=E2=80=9CIt=E2=80=99s funny because I have been a player for so long and th=
en transitioned to owning and operating the claw machines,=E2=80=9D he says=
. =E2=80=9CThere is a lot of psychology behind the playing. It triggers rew=
ard pathways. When you=E2=80=99re about to win something, you want to play =
again.=E2=80=9D

But becoming the operator ruined the magic. =E2=80=9CNow that I am on the o=
ther side, I know the machine is telling you when you=E2=80=99re going to w=
in. I don=E2=80=99t really play them anymore. It ruined the experience a li=
ttle bit.=E2=80=9D

Still, he believes in the product.

=E2=80=9CWe know we are providing a service: entertainment and fun. Even if=
 people don=E2=80=99t win, they have fun,=E2=80=9D he says.

A single machine can earn up to $400/week for Jack Ennis, who owns about 40=
 like these. (Courtesy of Jack Ennis)

Ennis=E2=80=99 business model is simple. According to him, machines cost be=
tween $500 and $1,000 and he believes that the return on the initial invest=
ment can likely be made in four to six months. After that, it=E2=80=99s pur=
e profit. Ennis says a single machine can earn him between $200 and $400/we=
ek.

Credit card readers exploded revenue (=E2=80=9CPeople lose track of spendin=
g with Apple Pay,=E2=80=9D Ennis says) while colorful prizes and pop-cultur=
e-themed toys keep engagement high. Add to it all locations with high foot =
traffic, and the potential for revenue keeps multiplying.

=E2=80=9CIt=E2=80=99s very scalable and profitable,=E2=80=9D says Ennis. =
=E2=80=9CA perfect side hustle.=E2=80=9D

But even here, the money isn=E2=80=99t about a single play. It=E2=80=99s th=
e ecosystem: nostalgia, psychology, social play, and the sheer volume of at=
tempts.

Meow Claw, a claw-machine arcade in Rockville, Maryland, is inspired by Jap=
an=E2=80=99s arcades, where the machines are called UFO catchers. (Photo by=
 Michael S. Williamson/The Washington Post via Getty Images)

And increasingly, content creators drive foot traffic as much as casual pla=
yers.

Content pays

There=E2=80=99s a misconception that claw-machine pros earn money the way g=
amers or athletes do: through competitions, high-stakes events, prize winni=
ngs. Both Kane and Magnone shut that down quickly.

There are small competitions popping up at dedicated =E2=80=9Cclawcades,=E2=
=80=9D they say, but nobody=E2=80=99s paying rent off a trophy.

The creators make money because they film themselves playing and sell ad in=
ventory against the millions of views that those clips generate.

Kane and Magnone also mention sponsorships, selling merchandise or the priz=
es that they won which, according to both, doesn=E2=80=99t actually amount =
to much.

To put it simply: the claw machine is the stage. The content is the product=
.

What started as a coin-eating arcade sideshow is now a full-blown ecosystem=
 powered by social media, nostalgia and the most consistent human impulse o=
f all: the desire to win. Or, at the very least, watch somebody else win.

