;"><em><span style=3D"font-size: 12px;">Matt Magnone says winning claw tour=
naments isn=E2=80=99t where the revenue is =E2=80=93 it=E2=80=99s in videos=
 of him playing the game. (Courtesy of Matt Magnone)</span></em></span></p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">And like any community, it has its own drama.=
 Magnone explains he=E2=80=99s called out members online who =E2=80=9Cgive =
false information on hacks=E2=80=9D to gather views.</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">=E2=80=9CThat ruins it for us content creator=
s because the arcades see us walking in with a camera and think we want to =
try to hack the system,=E2=80=9D he says.</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">He=E2=80=99s called out arcades, too, for all=
egedly <a href=3D"https://media.hubspot.com/e3t/Ctc/UB+113/d2q-Vf04/VVBCQ82=
QCH8lF6vjVJwtQX3W1FPBGm5J8y-jN11fKWg2-ZW0W69sMD-6lZ3nqW5n90PN4mtrLvW4mdMHm5=
tFxsxW40313B3p_K3KW527NYF90zfxkW7hmC9m5cxXKXW5QGJth2vWvlcW8npr603xBF4vW4YjD=
_v66TNwZW5sdtWH6nb65XW2y-54z6d8VrJVDK6FY9f_7HQW6BMhWQ7ZGJ9pW82DKQL2gcYnPVZ8=
mKg6z0NGrW5hhC1Q1ts6j1W7DYG-N2Bt3-JW5PBlm31w6VPqN34g7BF7nxfYW4slv737DVb4CW7=
clNlV3RyKBsf41CsP-04" style=3D"color:#192733" data-hs-link-id=3D"0" data-hs=
-link-id-v2=3D"uEWTj5Hv" target=3D"_blank">rigging their games</a> or keepi=
ng payout levels low. (Anecdotally, machines will pay out between <strong>1=
0%</strong>-<strong>30%</strong> of the time, but it depends on how the own=
er has set the game.)</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">Videos cost Magnone between <strong>$50</stro=
ng>-<strong>$100</strong>, and throughout his career he=E2=80=99s amassed a=
 wide range of prizes: from mp4 players (=E2=80=9Cthey were very valuable t=
hen=E2=80=9D) to licensed items like Minecraft-related products, giant Squi=
shable plushies and more. Once, he turned $20 into 33 prizes at Dave &amp; =
Busters.</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">=E2=80=9CI don=E2=80=99t really do any kind o=
f training,=E2=80=9D he says. =E2=80=9CClaw machines are one of those thing=
s that, once you get the feel for it, you just have to catch the machine at=
 the right time and the prize in the right position.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%"><strong>The jackpot behind the jackpot</stron=
g></p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">Neither Kane nor Magnone make money winning t=
ournament jackpots. The real opportunity, the thing that birthed what could=
 be described as the claw economy, is that claw machines translate perfectl=
y into content.</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">What makes claw-machine content so sticky onl=
ine is that it=E2=80=99s built on the same psychological loop as the gamepl=
ay itself: set up, suspense, and then payoff.</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">The viewer experiences the =E2=80=9Calmost wi=
n=E2=80=9D on screen, without paying for the next attempt, watching the cla=
w strains to latch onto the prize. It creates a kind of low-grade high: the=
 thrill of <em>almost</em>.</p></div></div></td></tr></tbody></table>
